.TH AENIGMA-UNLOCK-KEY 8 "2026-08-12" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-unlock-key \- remove passphrase encryption from the Aenigma
service's private key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-unlock-key
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-unlock-key
decrypts the Aenigma service's RSA private key,
.IR /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key.pem ,
using
.BR openssl (1),
replacing the passphrase-protected key with an
.B unencrypted, plaintext
copy. It takes no options or arguments.
.PP
The script performs the following actions:
.IP \(bu 2
Reads the existing private key from
.IR /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key.pem .
If the key is currently encrypted,
.BR openssl (1)
prompts interactively for its passphrase in order to decrypt it.
.IP \(bu 2
Writes a plaintext, unencrypted copy of the key to a temporary file,
.IR /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key\-unlocked.pem .
.IP \(bu 2
Replaces the original
.I private\-key.pem
with the unencrypted file.
.IP \(bu 2
Resets ownership to
.B aenigma:aenigma
and permissions to
.B 700
on the resulting file.
.PP
After this script completes,
.I private\-key.pem
is stored on disk
.B without
passphrase protection; anyone able to read the file (root, or the
.B aenigma
user) can use the key directly with no further authentication.
.PP
.B aenigma-unlock-key
must be run as root, and requires that
.I private\-key.pem
already exists.
.SH OPTIONS
.B aenigma-unlock-key
takes no command-line options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The private key was decrypted and stored successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The script was not run as root,
.I private\-key.pem
does not exist, or
.BR openssl (1)
failed (for example, an incorrect passphrase).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key.pem
The Aenigma service's RSA private key. Read to obtain the key
material, then overwritten in place with the decrypted, plaintext
version. Owned by
.B aenigma:aenigma
with mode
.B 700
after the operation.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private\-key\-unlocked.pem
Temporary file used to hold the decrypted key before it replaces
.IR private\-key.pem .
Not expected to persist after a successful run.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decrypt the private key, supplying its current passphrase when
prompted:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-unlock-key
.EE
.RE
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Running this script leaves the Aenigma service's private key as
.B plaintext on disk
until
.BR aenigma-lock-key (8)
is run again to re-encrypt it. Anyone who can read
.I private\-key.pem
during that window, or who obtains a copy of it (backup, snapshot,
compromised host access), gains full use of the key with no
passphrase required. This state should be treated as temporary and
minimized in duration; consider re-locking the key with
.BR aenigma-lock-key (8)
as soon as the operation requiring an unlocked key is complete.
.PP
The intermediate file
.I private\-key\-unlocked.pem
is created by
.BR openssl (1)
using the process's default permissions (governed by the calling
shell's umask), and is not restricted to mode
.B 700
until
.B after
it has replaced
.IR private\-key.pem .
Depending on the umask in effect when this script runs, the
plaintext private key may be briefly readable by users other than
.B aenigma
between its creation and the final
.BR chmod .
This is of particular concern here, since unlike
.BR aenigma-lock-key (8),
the exposed material during this window is unencrypted.
.PP
If the script is interrupted between writing
.I private\-key\-unlocked.pem
and the subsequent
.BR mv ,
an unencrypted copy of the private key may be left behind on disk
under the
.I private\-key\-unlocked.pem
filename, and will not be automatically detected or cleaned up on a
later run of this or any other Aenigma script.
.PP
This script takes no backup of the previous, encrypted
.I private\-key.pem
before overwriting it with the plaintext version.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openssl (1),
.BR aenigma-lock-key (8),
.BR aenigma-keys (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.ruja@aenigma.ro>
